# The GOLF/2 bootstrap seed, written in v1 GOLF.
#
# THIS IS A SOURCE FILE.  It carries the same compiler as self/golf2.golfj,
# written in the language v1 could read, purely so v1c — which has never heard
# of v2 — can build something able to compile golf2.golf.  Same token dispatch
# order, same backpatch discipline, same emitted bytes for every input;
# test/selfcheck.sh gates that byte for byte, so a change to compiler logic must
# land in BOTH this file and self/golf2.golfj or selfcheck goes red.
#
# Build: tools/mkblob2.py strips the comments and whitespace below and splices
# the template blob in at the @BLOB@ marker (a backtick, the byte count, one
# space, the raw bytes), producing the GENERATED self/seed.golf.  Never
# hand-edit that file; run `python3 tools/mkblob2.py` from expanded/.
#
#     v1c -> seed (self/seed.golf) -> golf2 (self/golf2.golf) -> golf2'
#                                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
#                                     fixpoint: golf2 == golf2'
#
# v1c is the frozen minimal compiler.  It is not built from source here any
# more: it is a pinned release binary, downloaded and sha256-checked by
# tools/seed.sh per the ../SEEDS pin.  The minimal tree it was built from lives
# on the `minimal` branch (tag v1.0-minimal).
#
# Lexically this is v1 GOLF with two additions this file's reader handles, both
# stripped before v1c ever sees them: `#` comments run to end of line, and
# whitespace is insignificant (the one load-bearing space, after the blob's byte
# count, is inside the @BLOB@ splice).
#
# Everything up to the `#` in each line is one v1-GOLF word or token case, in
# definition-before-use order.  The single-letter accessor words defined on the
# first line (P B S Q U Y Z, and L below) are the size-reducing rewrites v1's
# own build applied: P = m4+@ the output pointer, B = 4800512 the output buffer
# (0x494000 since W11-1E, 192 KB of program; v1's m4096+ held only 64 KB),
# S = m12+ scratch, Q = m16+ the pushed-back char, U = 48-10< the is-digit test,
# Y = 4*4792320+ the dict slot for the key on top (0x492000 + 4*name since
# W10-A; v1's was m2048+\4*+, which had room for 512 names), Z = 48-\10*+ fold a decimal
# digit, L = 104o emit the push-imm32 opcode.
#
# FROZEN IN SHAPE.  This is the bootstrap rung and nothing more; new compiler
# features go in self/golf2.golfj, and only reach here when they must (a case
# the seed has to understand to compile golf2.golf).  See ../DESIGN.md.

:m4259840;:Pm4+@;:B4800512;:Sm12+;:Qm16+;:U48-10<;:Y4*4792320+;:Z48-\10*+; # m: data base D, then the accessors
:am4+\&@+\!P4997121<[2.];                               # a: advance P by n -- past the 192 KB cap (P > B+196608): exit 2 (W11-1E)
:oP!1a;:L104o;                                          # o: emit byte  ·  L: emit push-imm32 (0x68)
:wP!4a;                                                 # w: emit dword
:k"P\-4-\!;                                             # k: backpatch pop site
:c{\"?o1+\1-"1<}__;                                     # c: copy n bytes -> out
:C"1+?\2+\c;                                            # C: copy template @rec
:fS!m8+@{"?"[_^]S@-[^]"1+?2++0};                        # f: find record for key
:EfC;                                                   # E: emit template for key
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# M2 — long names, ‹name› (0xB4 … 0xB5).  A long name is interned to a NAME
# NUMBER 256+index at its first appearance, and from then on is a name that
# does not fit a byte: Y (dict at 0x492000 + 4*name) and the variable bank
# (0x4E0000 + 8*name) take it with their one-byte formulas.  Tables, both
# compiler-only (../REGISTRY.md §3):
#     0x47C000 + 64*E   entry for name E (256..1279): [len:4][up to 60 bytes]
#     0x490400 + 4*N    ANY name N's forward-reference head (one-byte names
#                       too, since W10-A): the last unpatched site
# Seed-side scratch:  m60 name count · m64 length being read · m68 entry being
# read into · m72 entry compared against · m76 compare countdown · m80 index ·
# m84 a body address.  (self/golf2.golfj names the same state ‹count› ‹len›
# ‹entry› ‹other› ‹left› ‹idx› in its own variable bank.)
#
# Every error here is golf2's `GOLF: bad long name` / `too many long names` /
# `undefined word ‹name›` — and, as for `e`, the seed can only exit(2).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:xm64+@4+m76+!0{_m76+@[0^]m76+@1-m76+!m72+@m76+@+?m68+@m76+@+?-"}; # x: 0 iff entries m72 and m68 hold one name (length cell and bytes, last to first)
# N: read a long name after ‹ straight into the next free entry; on › look for
# it among the names so far, else claim that entry.  -> its name number.  A name
# byte must be 0-9 A-Z a-z _ or - : the five range tests sum to -1 for exactly
# one of them and to 0 for anything else, EOF included.
# W11-4A split N into the pieces ⊜ needs too, since a record invents names
# (‹point-x›, ‹set-point-x›) and interns them as if read: n starts a name in the
# next free entry, d is the name-byte test (ch -> ch sum: -1 legal, 0 not), b
# appends a byte (past 60: exit 2), i interns the finished name -> its number.
:nm60+@256+64*4702208+m68+!0m64+!;                       # n: start a name in the next free entry
:d"48-10<&65-26<+&97-26<+&95-1<+&45-1<+;                 # d: ch -> ch f, f = -1 for 0-9 A-Z a-z _ -
:bm64+@60<[2.]m68+@4+m64+@+,m64+@1+m64+!;                # b: ch -> ; append it to the name
:im64+@m68+@!m64+@[2.]0m80+!{m80+@m60+@-[m60+@1024<[2.]m60+@"1+m60+!256+^]m80+@256+64*4702208+m72+!x[m80+@256+^]m80+@1+m80+!0}; # i: -> the built name's number, interned
:Nn{("181-[_i^]d[2.]b0};
:G("180-[_N];                                           # G: a prefix's operand -- one byte, or a whole ‹long name›
:F\o4*4785152+"@P\w\!;                                  # F: op name -> emit `op rel32`, the site joining name's pending list
:R"Y@m84+!4*4785152+"@\0\!{"[_^]"@\"m84+@\-4-\!0};       # R: name -> patch its pending sites to its (just stored) body
:z0m80+!{m80+@m60+@256+-[^]m80+@4*4785152+@1<[2.]m80+@1+m80+!0}; # z: end of input -- any name (one-byte or long) still pending is undefined
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# W11-2A — include, ⎘“path“ (0xB6).  The compiler reads the file's bytes as if
# they stood where the ⎘ is, by swapping fd 0: dup(0) saves the includer's
# input, open + dup2 puts the file on fd 0, and at its end j dup2s the saved fd
# back (J).  Search: the path as written, then lib/path, then vendor/path (W11-4A).  Include-once: a path
# already included (same bytes) is skipped.  At most 8 deep, 60 files.  Layout
# (compiler-only, ../REGISTRY.md §3): 0x438000 [len:4][path][0] (the len cell
# becomes `lib/` for the retry) · 0x438100 the saved-fd stack, 4 bytes a level
# · 0x438200 the table, 256 bytes an entry, [len:4][bytes] so x compares them.
# Seed-side scratch: m160 depth · m164 table count · m168 the fd · m172 the
# copy countdown; the path's length is m64 and x's entries m68/m72, M2's
# scratch, idle between tokens.  (self/golf2.golfj: ‹depth› ‹includes› ‹fd›.)
#
# v1 GOLF has no syscall op, so K is one: its body is a raw blob escape holding
# ⎈'s template plus `jmp +5` (tools/mkblob2.py splices SEED_SYS in at @SYS@).
# As compiled, K jumps over the blob and returns the blob's address; main calls
# it once at startup and zeroes that jmp's rel32 (the code segment is RWX), and
# from then on K falls into the blob: a1 a2 a3 num -> result, like ⎈.  O is K
# with a2 = a3 = 0, V the path's address.  Every failure exits 2 silently; golf2
# says `GOLF: cannot include <path>` (and the like) on fd 2.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:K@SYS@;:O0\0\K;:V4423684;                              # K: syscall (armed at startup) · O: a1 num -> r · V: the path
:Jm160+@1-m160+!m160+@4*4423936+@m168+!m168+@33O_m168+@3O_; # J: leave an include -- dup2(saved,0), close(saved)
:j0{_("[m160+@[^]J]"};                                  # j: the next input byte; an included file's end resumes its includer
:r0m64+!{("142-[_^]"[2.]m64+@250<[2.]m64+@V+,m64+@1+m64+!0}; # r: read the path up to the closing “ (EOF, or past 250 bytes: exit 2)
# W: W11-4A's third place, vendor/path.  `vendor/` is 7 bytes, more than the
# length cell holds, so the C string is built at 0x43F000 (W11-4A's region):
# `vend` and `or/`, then the path and its NUL copied from V (m172 counts).
:W1684956534m192512+!3109487m192516+!0m172+!{Vm172+@+?m192519+m172+@+,m172+@1+"m172+!m64+@1+-1<}m192512+2O"1048576<[2.];
# I: the ⎘ case.  “ must follow; read the path, NUL-terminate it, give it its
# length cell; skip it if the table has it; else check depth and count, copy it
# into the table, open it (or lib/path, or vendor/path), save fd 0 and dup2 the file onto it.
:I(142-1<[2.]r0m64+@V+,m64+@V4-!V4-m68+!0m80+!{m80+@m164+@-1<"[m80+@256*4424192+m72+!x[_^]m80+@1+m80+!]}m160+@8<[2.]m164+@60<[2.]m164+@256*4424192+m72+!m64+@4+m172+!{m172+@1-m172+!V4-m172+@+?m72+@m172+@+,m172+@1<}m164+@1+m164+!V2O"1048576<[_794978668V4-!V4-2O"1048576<[_W]]m168+!0""32Km160+@4*4423936+!m160+@1+m160+!m168+@33O_m168+@3O_;
# e — the default: emit a table op, or compile a call to a user word.
#
# v1's `e` emitted `call rel32` with rel32 = dict[name] - (P+4) and never looked
# at dict[name].  For a name that was never defined that cell is BSS-zero, so the
# call targeted a wild address: the compile reported success and the binary died
# with SIGSEGV when the word was reached.  Both compilers now test the slot first
# (M-DIAG) and, since W10-A, call through the name's pending list instead, so z
# can exit(2) at end of input if the name is never defined.
#
# ASYMMETRY, deliberate and gated by test/selfcheck.sh: golf2 also writes
# `GOLF: undefined word <name>` to fd 2, and this seed does not.  It cannot —
# this source is v1 GOLF, compiled by the frozen v1c, whose entire output surface
# is `)` (one byte to fd 1) and `.` (exit).  There is no way to reach fd 2 from
# v1 GOLF, and fd 1 is carrying the binary.  The two compilers therefore agree on
# every byte they EMIT and on the exit status they reject with; they differ only
# in whether the rejection is explained.  selfcheck.sh asserts both halves.  The
# seed's error path is also unreachable in the build: its one job is to compile
# self/golf2.golf, which is valid by construction.
#
# The reordering against v1 is load-bearing: `232o` has to sit BELOW the lookup
# so the slot can be tested before a byte is written, and `P` is read after
# `232o` in both forms, so rel32 is unchanged.
# M2 / W10-A: with dict[name] zero, a name is not an error yet but a forward
# call through its pending list (F) — a long name since M2, a one-byte name
# since W10-A; z rejects whatever is still pending at end of input.
:e"f"?[_"Y@"[_232\F^]\_232oP4+-w^]\_C;                 # e: table op or call (undefined word -> forward, checked by z)
:D0{("U[_^]Z0};                                         # D: read decimal
:gQ@"[_j^]0Q!;                                          # g: get char (pending Q); a fresh one through j (W11-2A)
:h{("10-[_^]"[_^]_0};                                   # h: skip comment line
# X — push the address of the word or atom named by the byte on top.
#
# If the name is a defined word, push its VA.  If it is an ATOM (a template in
# the blob, not a word), emit a thunk [prologue][template][epilogue] inline,
# jumped over, and push the thunk's VA — so ′+ works without wrapping.  Compiler
# scratch: m20 the jmp-site, m24 the thunk address.
#
# This is the body of the `′` case, lifted into a word of its own by M-CHAIN2,
# which needs the very same "push the address of the word or atom named by this
# byte" three times over.  It has to be defined before `t` (which calls it) and
# after everything it calls itself — o, w, E and the templates — which `h` is the
# last of, so it lands exactly here.
#
# M2 / W10-A: a name that is neither a word nor an atom (a long name always
# is not an atom: f finds no record for it) may be defined further down, so ′
# pushes the address of a thunk [jmp rel32] instead (jumped over), the rel32 on
# the name's pending list; ⍎ on the thunk tail-jumps into the word.  If it never
# comes (`′typo`), z rejects it at end of input with exit(2).  Before W9-1C it
# was wrapped like an atom, around a template that does not exist (SIGILL).
:X"Y@"[_"f?[233o5wPm24+!233&FLm24+@606208-w_^]233oPm20+!0wPm24+!1E"E94EPm20+@4+-m20+@!Lm24+@606208-w_^]\_606208-Lw; # X: emit a push of a word's address
# l — M-FRAME2: the rbp displacement of a local, from its name number.  A
# one-byte local must be a..z (W12-Z3; a..h before).  Outside a ⊡ word (m56
# clear) it keeps M-FRAME's fixed slot name-'a', so there only a..h pass, and a
# long name is refused.  Inside, the definition's
# slot table at m96 (name numbers, 4 bytes each) is searched in order of first
# appearance; a new name claims slot m88 (the count so far), at most 16, so every
# displacement is a disp8.  m80 is the walking index (M2's, idle by now).  golf2
# names the offending local on fd 2; the seed can only exit(2).
:l"256<1+["97-26<[2.]]m56+@["256<[2.]"97-8<[2.]97-8*^]0m80+!{m80+@m88+@-[m88+@16<[2.]"m88+@4*m96++!_m88+@"1+m88+!8*^]"m80+@4*m96++@-[_m80+@8*^]m80+@1+m80+!0}; # l: name -> local displacement
# W12-Z2 — ⇡x (0xB9) / ⇣x (0xBA): inc / dec qword [0x4E0000+8x] in place, or
# [rbp+disp] when this ⊡ word has already named x as a local (M walks l's table
# without claiming a slot: name -> name slot+1, or name 0).  T: k -> ; k is 0
# for inc, 8 for dec (the modrm's /0 or /1).  x must be a letter or a long
# name; golf2 says `GOLF: ⇡ needs a variable`, the seed exits 2.
:Mm56+@1-[0m80+!{m80+@m88+@-[0^]"m80+@4*m96++@-[m80+@1+^]m80+@1+m80+!0}]0; # M: name -> name s
:TG"256<1+["65-26<&97-26<+[2.]]M"[_72o255o\4+o37o8*5111808+w^]1-8*\_72o255o\69+oo; # T: k -> ; ⇡/⇣'s bytes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# W11-4A — records, ⊜‹point›‹x›‹y›; (0xB7).  At compile time the header becomes
# the words golf2's ‹defrecord› emits (see self/golf2.golfj, Records):
#     :‹point› n⍳ then per field k, last first: \&16≪16>(8k+8)﹢⊛ ;
#     :‹point-x› k⊇;    :‹set-point-x› &k⊇_&16≪16>(8k+8)﹢⊛;
# Seed-side scratch (../REGISTRY.md §3): m192 the record's name number · m196
# the field count · m200 the field being emitted · m204 u's byte pointer; u's
# countdown at 0x43FF00 and the field table (4 bytes a name) at 0x43C000, the
# W11-4A region.  Every malformed header is golf2's `GOLF: record …` / `bad
# record …`; the seed exits 2.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:u"256<[64*4702208+"4+m204+!@4456192!{m204+@?bm204+@1+m204+!4456192@1-"4456192!1<}^]b; # u: name -> ; append its spelling
:q"233oP4+\Y!RP0w1E;                                     # q: name -> site; `:name`'s header
:s38e16Lw162e16Lw62e1+8*Lw145e165e;                      # s: k -> ; emit &16≪16>(8k+8)﹢⊛
:v0m196+!{("59-[_m196+@[2.]^]"[2.]"33<["180-[_N]"256<1+[d[2.]]m196+@256<[2.]m196+@4*4440064+!m196+@1+m196+!0]_0}; # v: read the fields up to ;
:yG"256<1+[d[2.]]m192+!vm192+@qm196+@Lw82em196+@m200+!{m200+@1-"m200+!92esm200+@1<}94Ek0m200+!{nm192+@u45bm200+@4*4440064+@uiqm200+@Lw73e94Ekn115b101b116b45bm192+@u45bm200+@4*4440064+@uiq38em200+@Lw73e95em200+@s94Ekm200+@1+"m200+!m196+@-1<}; # y: the ⊜ case
:t"33<1+[_^]                                            # whitespace
"35-[_h^]                                               # '#' comment
"U1+[48-{("U[Q!Lw^]Z0}]                                 # digit -> number
"39-[_(Lw^]                                             # "'" char literal
"58-[_G"233oP4+\Y!RP0w1E^]                                # ':' define word
# `;` closes a chain and a frame as well as a definition: a 1- or 2-link chain
# never reached the fork, so its links are emitted here as plain calls, and a ⊡
# word releases its frame.  Then the ordinary epilogue and backpatch, which are
# the same for every kind of definition.
"59-[_m32+@1-[0m32+!m36+@1-[m40+@e]m36+@2-[m40+@em44+@e]]m56+@1-[0m56+!{m92+@@m88+@8*m92+@!m92+!m92+@1<}72o129o197om88+@8*w]94Ek^] # ';' end def -- and close a chain
# W12-A7: `[` and `{` push a tag above their address (golf2.golfj's dispatcher
# header has the table): 1000001 `[`, 1000002 `{`, 1000003 after `[…⋄`,
# 1000004 after `{c⋄` (the exit site sits between it and the target).  `]`
# drops whichever tag; `}` after a 1000004 jumps back unconditionally and lands
# the exit (S holds it a moment).
"91-[_2E133oP0w1000001^]                                # '[' if
"93-[__k^]                                              # ']' end if (or else)
"'{-[_P1000002^]                                        # '{' loop start ('{ = 123)
"'}-[_"1000004-[_S!233oP4+-wS@k^]_2E132oP4+-w^]         # '}' loop end  ('} = 125), or a while's
"96-[_D233o"wP606208-S!{(o1-"1<}_LS@w^]                  # '`' blob
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything from here to the `e;` fallthrough is v2's, not v1's: the compiler
# cases the seed has to understand because self/golf2.golfj uses them.  Each is
# written in pure v1 GOLF, because v1c is what compiles this file.  Byte
# allocations come from ../REGISTRY.md; tools/mkblob2.py owns the constants.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ′ (0x8C) — a quotation: a compiler PREFIX.  Read the next byte, a word name,
# and emit `push <that word's runtime address>` instead of a call.  This is the
# first change to compiler logic over v1, and what makes higher-order functions
# possible: ′f pushes a callable and ⍎ calls it, so map and fold work.
"140-[_GX^]                                             # 0x8C  ′  quotation: push a word's address

# “ (0x8E) — a string literal.  “...“ emits a length-prefixed byte block
# [len(4)][bytes], jumped over, and pushes its address.  A string is thus a byte
# block; `chars` converts it to a list of codes so every list op applies.
# Compiler scratch: m20 jmp-site, m24 len-site, S push-VA, m28 count.
# golf2's own source never uses this — it is here for the programs golf2 compiles.
"142-[_233oPm20+!0wPm24+!P606208-S!0w0m28+!{("142-[_m28+@m24+@!Pm20+@4+-m20+@!LS@w^]om28+@1+m28+!0}] # 0x8E  “  string literal -> inline byte block

# →x (0x8F) / ←x (0x90) — named variables.  Compiler prefixes: read the name
# byte and emit an absolute 64-bit store/load against a per-program variable
# bank at 0x4E0000, where name c owns the cell at 0x4E0000 + 8*c.
#
# These are GLOBAL — one cell per name, not per call — so recursion does not get
# fresh copies.  That is what ⊡/⇒/⇐ below exist to fix, and why both spellings
# survive: `←a←b+←a←b-*` is still the right way to say (a+b)*(a-b) at top level.
#
# M3W: the bank was 4 bytes per name and `←x` was a `mov eax`, which zero-
# extended — `4294967296→x←x` came back 0, and `0 5-→x←x` came back 4294967291.
# Every other place a value can sit had been 64 bits since M4/W4A; this was the
# last narrow one.  The fix is stride 4 -> 8 and a REX.W on both templates.
# 256 names x 8 bytes = 2 KB, still inside the 0x4E0000-0x4F0000 hole, so no
# address had to be reallocated.  Widening in place was impossible for the same
# reason the scratch bank could not be: at a 4-byte stride x's high half IS the
# cell of name x+1.
"143-[_G8*5111808+88o72o137o4o37ow^]                    # 0x8F  →x store TOS to the global variable bank
"144-[_G8*5111808+72o139o4o37ow80o^]                    # 0x90  ←x load from the global variable bank

# ⊡ (0xB1) / ⇒x (0xB2) / ⇐x (0xB3) — M-FRAME, per-call locals, sized per
# definition since M-FRAME2 (W9-4D).
#
#   ⊡name … ;   define a word WITH a frame of locals, one cell per local named
#   ⇒x          pop TOS into local x   (x in a..z, or a ‹long name›)
#   ⇐x          push local x
#
# ⊡ is `:`'s case plus three things: `sub rbp, imm32` right after the prologue,
# its immediate left unpatched; a flag in m56 saying "this definition has a
# frame", which `^` and `;` read; and a zeroed local count in m88.  Layout, for
# a word naming k locals, with R the address the return address is stored at:
#
#     [rbp+0] .. [rbp+8k-8]  the k slots, in order of first appearance
#     [rbp+8k] = R           the return address
#
# `l` gives a name its slot (m96 holds the table), so ⇒/⇐ stay five bytes; only
# k waits for `;`.  Every frame-size immediate — the ⊡'s and each `^`'s
# `add rbp, imm32` — holds the previous one's address (the ⊡'s holds 0) and m92
# the newest, so `;` walks that list writing 8k into each, then emits its own
# `add rbp, 8k`.  A one-letter local past z (past h outside a ⊡ word), a 17th
# local, or a long local outside a ⊡ word stops the compile: golf2 says which on fd 2; this seed, in v1
# GOLF, can only exit(2).  Same status, same asymmetry as `e` above, gated by
# test/selfcheck.sh.
"177-[_G"233oP4+\Y!RP0w1E72o129o237oPm92+!0w0m88+!1m56+!^] # 0xB1  ⊡  define a word owning a frame of locals
"178-[_Gl88o72o137o69oo^]                               # 0xB2  ⇒x pop TOS into local x (a..z or ‹name›)
"179-[_Gl72o139o69oo80o^]                               # 0xB3  ⇐x push local x
"185-[_0T^]                                             # 0xB9  ⇡x increment variable x in place (W12-Z2)
"186-[_8T^]                                             # 0xBA  ⇣x decrement it

# `^` was a plain template until M-FRAME; it becomes a case purely so it can test
# that flag (and, since M-FRAME2, link its frame-size immediate into m92's
# list).  With the flag clear it emits `94E` — the same template `e` would
# have copied — so every existing program compiles to the bytes it did before,
# which is what keeps the ladder and the fixpoint undisturbed.
"94-[_m56+@1-[72o129o197om92+@Pm92+!w]94E^]               # '^' early return: release the frame first when there is one

# ⊚ (0xB0) — M-CHAIN2, a CHAIN definition.  `⊚name f g h;` is the tacit train the
# explicit spelling builds, with the compiler supplying every ′:
#
#     links   the chain is                       compiles to
#     0       identity                           nothing
#     1       f x                                a plain call
#     2       g(f x)          — atop             two plain calls
#     3       h(f x, g x)     — a fork           ′f ′g ′h ⑂
#     n>3     the fork, then the rest in turn    ′f ′g ′h ⑂ then plain calls
#
# So the count decides the shape and only the first three link bytes have to be
# buffered: the third emits the three pushes and the ⑂ call (byte 199, an
# ordinary prelude word — `199e` compiles the call to it), a fourth or later link
# is an ordinary token, and `;` flushes a 1- or 2-link chain.  No driver word, no
# new prelude state, no runtime list of quotations.
#
# The header is `:`'s, plus the two state cells: jmp over the body, dict[name] =
# body address, leave the jmp site on the stack for `;`, emit the prologue.
# Seed-side state, ../REGISTRY.md §3:  m32 in-a-chain flag · m36 link count ·
# m40/m44/m48 the buffered link bytes.  (self/golf2.golfj holds the same five in
# its variable bank as u K F G I — it owns that bank; see its header.)
"176-[_G"233oP4+\Y!RP0w1E1m32+!0m36+!^]                   # 0xB0  ⊚  chain definition header

# ‹ (0xB4) as a TOKEN: read and intern the long name, and let its number take
# the char's place and fall through — so the LINK case and `e` below treat it
# exactly like a one-byte name.
# ⋄ (0xB8, W12-A7) — in a `[`: jmp to `]` and send the `[`'s jnz to the else
# branch; in a `{`: pop c and leave the loop unless it is 0.  Anywhere else
# golf2 says `GOLF: ⋄ outside [ ] or { }` (or `twice in one block`); the seed exits 2.
"184-[_"1000001-[_233oP0w\k1000003^]"1000002-[_2E133oP0w1000004^]2.] # 0xB8  ⋄  else / while test
"182-[_I^]                                              # 0xB6  ⎘  include a file (W11-2A)
"183-[_y^]                                              # 0xB7  ⊜  a record header (W11-4A)
"180-[_N]                                               # 0xB4  ‹  a long name used as a token

# A token inside a chain is a LINK.  This case must sit LAST, just before the
# `e;` fallthrough: everything `t` recognizes earlier — whitespace, comments,
# digits, the structural bytes — keeps its ordinary meaning inside a chain body.
m32+@1-[m36+@1+m36+!m36+@1-[m40+!^]m36+@2-[m44+!^]m36+@3-[m48+!m40+@Xm44+@Xm48+@X199e^]e^] # LINK: buffer or emit a chain link
e;                                                      # default: e
# H: write the ELF header from (offset,value) pairs (record key 4) over the
# zeroed output buffer.  end kept in S; store each value byte at B+offset.
:H4f"1+?\2+\&+S!{"?B+&1+?\,2+"S@<1+}_;

# The template blob, spliced in here by tools/mkblob2.py.
@BLOB@

# main: store the blob address, arm K (zero the rel32 of its jmp over the
# syscall blob, W11-2A), write the ELF header, set P = B+120, emit
# the startup stub, run the token loop, check no long name is left undefined
# (z), exit, write the image size into p_filesz (header offset 96, W11-1E),
# then flush the output buffer.
m8+!K4-0\!HB120+m4+!3E{g"m!tm@1<}z5EPB-B96+!B{"?)1+"P<1+}_
